774
00:30:25,803 --> 00:30:26,902
It's like cream sauce.

775
00:30:26,903 --> 00:30:27,903
- Really? - It's thick, right?

776
00:30:27,903 --> 00:30:28,903
It's like fresh cream.

777
00:30:29,703 --> 00:30:30,703
(Sea urchin roe is a rare ingredient...)

778
00:30:30,704 --> 00:30:31,872
(since 1 sea urchin only yields 5 to 10g of roe.)

779
00:30:31,873 --> 00:30:33,212
(It's characterized by a clean taste with the aroma of the sea.)

780
00:30:33,213 --> 00:30:36,483
Sea urchins are harmful to the sea.

781
00:30:36,643 --> 00:30:37,643
- Sea urchins? - Yes.

782
00:30:37,644 --> 00:30:39,412
- Really? - Sea urchins ruin the sea.

783
00:30:39,413 --> 00:30:40,582
- Really? - Don't they help the sea?

784
00:30:40,583 --> 00:30:41,953
- They eat seaweed. - Yes.

785
00:30:42,153 --> 00:30:44,299
They're very tasty because they eat all the good ingredients.

786
00:30:44,323 --> 00:30:46,092
There are so many sea urchins inside the sea right now.

787
00:30:46,093 --> 00:30:47,222
- So they need to be harvested. - They multiply in summer.

788
00:30:47,223 --> 00:30:49,262
- Yes. They need to be harvested. - Let's eat them up.

789
00:30:49,263 --> 00:30:51,303
(Let's eat up the sea urchins for the environment.)

790
00:30:51,693 --> 00:30:52,993
I'll give you a spoonful.

791
00:30:53,533 --> 00:30:54,693
- Shall we go back? - Yes.

792
00:30:56,203 --> 00:30:58,403
(Captain Ha sails the boat on their way back as well.)

793
00:31:00,773 --> 00:31:02,433
(They set off after snacking on seafood.)

794
00:31:03,673 --> 00:31:05,172
(You're good at sailing the boat.)

795
00:31:05,173 --> 00:31:06,313
He always praises his wife.

796
00:31:06,843 --> 00:31:08,013
Seriously.

797
00:31:09,243 --> 00:31:10,543
This is nice.

798
00:31:11,683 --> 00:31:12,752
Gulls!

799
00:31:12,753 --> 00:31:14,812
(Gulls!)

800
00:31:14,813 --> 00:31:15,953
Gulls!

801
00:31:17,823 --> 00:31:21,752
(They pass by a colony of gulls...)

802
00:31:21,753 --> 00:31:23,593
(and enter the port.)

803
00:31:24,463 --> 00:31:26,793
Jae Suk, you've become a fishing villager.

804
00:31:27,293 --> 00:31:28,493
- What? - Seriously.

805
00:31:29,403 --> 00:31:32,503
Once the boat reaches the port, it'll bump into the edge.

806
00:31:32,673 --> 00:31:34,973
Then secure it with a rope.

807
00:31:35,133 --> 00:31:36,173
Okay.

808
00:31:36,703 --> 00:31:40,043
(He stands at the front to secure the rope.)

809
00:31:40,643 --> 00:31:41,672
Go slowly.

810
00:31:41,673 --> 00:31:43,882
Hold onto something. The boat will bump into the edge.

811
00:31:43,883 --> 00:31:45,343
This is scary. Jae Suk.

812
00:31:47,213 --> 00:31:48,312
- Oh, my. - My goodness.

813
00:31:48,313 --> 00:31:49,323
(My goodness)

814
00:31:50,423 --> 00:31:52,353
(It's nothing to a fishing villager.)

815
00:31:54,993 --> 00:31:56,263
Jae Suk, where are you going?

816
00:31:57,123 --> 00:31:59,533
(Calm)

817
00:32:03,933 --> 00:32:05,333
She parked it well.

818
00:32:06,173 --> 00:32:07,272
- That's right. - Did you secure it?

819
00:32:07,273 --> 00:32:08,702
- She parked it very well. - I did it.

820
00:32:08,703 --> 00:32:10,672
You're supposed to do it in a tough way.

821
00:32:10,673 --> 00:32:11,803
Well done.

822
00:32:13,573 --> 00:32:14,919
I don't understand why I'm starving.

823
00:32:14,943 --> 00:32:16,712
Why am I so hungry? I haven't done much.

824
00:32:16,713 --> 00:32:19,043
- They haven't eaten a lot. - Let's rinse the gear.

825
00:32:19,553 --> 00:32:20,993
I don't understand why I'm starving.

826
00:32:21,683 --> 00:32:22,823
Jang Woo, rinse the gear.

827
00:32:23,123 --> 00:32:25,022
- It's time to rinse the gear. - You do it right away.

828
00:32:25,023 --> 00:32:26,693
Yes. Otherwise, it becomes rusty.

829
00:32:27,023 --> 00:32:28,753
Salt makes things rusty.

830
00:32:30,123 --> 00:32:32,523
(They quickly put away the oxygen tanks.)

831
00:32:34,163 --> 00:32:36,002
- I'm starving. - Let's wash up quickly and eat.

832
00:32:36,003 --> 00:32:37,063
Okay.

833
00:32:38,003 --> 00:32:40,673
(He walks over to somewhere.)

834
00:32:43,573 --> 00:32:45,703
(He opens the trunk.)

835
00:32:48,813 --> 00:32:50,682
(He brought things from Seoul.)

836
00:32:50,683 --> 00:32:53,313
He brought many things.

837
00:32:53,453 --> 00:32:54,513
I'm hungry.

838
00:32:54,813 --> 00:32:56,023
I'm hungry too.

839
00:32:56,553 --> 00:32:58,283
Seriously. Why am I so hungry?

840
00:32:58,923 --> 00:32:59,952
- Jang Woo. - Yes?

841
00:32:59,953 --> 00:33:01,999
There isn't anything special. We just have a bigfin reef squid.

842
00:33:02,023 --> 00:33:03,492
- Jun Haeng caught it. - What? A bigfin reef squid?

843
00:33:03,493 --> 00:33:05,322
- He caught it and froze it. - A bigfin reef squid?

844
00:33:05,323 --> 00:33:06,363
It's a bigfin reef squid.

845
00:33:06,463 --> 00:33:08,093
(Big)

846
00:33:08,533 --> 00:33:09,703
It's rare.

847
00:33:09,833 --> 00:33:10,932
I've never heard of it before.

848
00:33:10,933 --> 00:33:12,613
- I've never heard of it before. - Really?

849
00:33:12,803 --> 00:33:14,703
How much is a bigfin reef squid?

850
00:33:14,833 --> 00:33:16,602
- It depends on the season. - That's right. It depends.

851
00:33:16,603 --> 00:33:19,073
- Yes. They can't be farmed. - No, they can't be.

852
00:33:19,203 --> 00:33:22,273
- That one was this big. - I've never seen one so big.

853
00:33:22,883 --> 00:33:23,982
He caught it this morning.

854
00:33:23,983 --> 00:33:25,013
These are whelks.

855
00:33:25,383 --> 00:33:26,882
They're tasty when you boil them in water.

856
00:33:26,883 --> 00:33:28,203
- Whelks. - They're really tasty.

857
00:33:28,453 --> 00:33:30,213
- This one was caught today too. - This one?

858
00:33:30,853 --> 00:33:31,982
- What's that? - An octopus.

859
00:33:31,983 --> 00:33:33,192
My goodness.

860
00:33:33,193 --> 00:33:34,793
Let me see its face.

861
00:33:36,093 --> 00:33:38,022
- Oh, my. It's coming out. - My goodness.

862
00:33:38,023 --> 00:33:39,193
- It's alive. - It's alive.

863
00:33:39,593 --> 00:33:41,593
- It's big. - It's big, right?

864
00:33:41,793 --> 00:33:42,962
Watch out for the ink.

865
00:33:42,963 --> 00:33:44,732
- It's the tasty size. - You have to eat it.

866
00:33:44,733 --> 00:33:46,703
- Shall we boil some water? - Okay.

867
00:33:46,903 --> 00:33:48,203
I... Hey.

868
00:33:48,933 --> 00:33:50,043
Go back inside.

869
00:33:51,673 --> 00:33:53,793
- I'll bring a burner. - We need a cutting board too.

870
00:33:56,183 --> 00:33:57,343
He's light on his feet.

871
00:33:57,743 --> 00:33:59,853
- He'll get sick. - He's working again.

872
00:34:01,253 --> 00:34:03,853
(He helps with everything.)

873
00:34:03,953 --> 00:34:06,273
(The world's number 1 man who is the lightest on his feet)

874
00:34:09,023 --> 00:34:10,093
Towels.

875
00:34:10,863 --> 00:34:12,323
He brought condiments.

876
00:34:12,863 --> 00:34:15,063
- Ice... - Jang Woo, shall we steam it?

877
00:34:16,333 --> 00:34:18,832
- We could steam it as it is. - It'll be so tasty.

878
00:34:18,833 --> 00:34:19,973
- Right? - Let's do that.

879
00:34:20,573 --> 00:34:22,433
- That sounds good. - Gosh, this is...

880
00:34:22,533 --> 00:34:26,013
I had originally planned to make stir-fried chicken and seafood...

881
00:34:26,113 --> 00:34:28,073
as well as seafood and jjajang rice.

882
00:34:28,373 --> 00:34:30,543
But since the weather was so nice,

883
00:34:30,843 --> 00:34:33,352
the captains gave us a squid...

884
00:34:33,353 --> 00:34:34,683
and an octopus.

885
00:34:34,913 --> 00:34:37,352
- There was no place for chicken. - That's right.

886
00:34:37,353 --> 00:34:38,353
(People at the sea village are so generous.)

887
00:34:38,354 --> 00:34:40,493
I plan to cook the squid as it is in the water.

888
00:34:40,893 --> 00:34:42,762
I want to make seafood jjajang.

889
00:34:42,763 --> 00:34:44,263
- Jjajang sounds amazing. - Yes.

890
00:34:44,993 --> 00:34:47,313
- How should we cook the squid? - Let's put it in jjajang.

891
00:34:47,493 --> 00:34:49,933
- Will you add a bigfin reef squid? - It's buttery and tasty.

892
00:34:50,363 --> 00:34:51,832
- A bigfin reef squid? - What do you think?

893
00:34:51,833 --> 00:34:53,272
- Bigfin reef squid jjajang? - What?

894
00:34:53,273 --> 00:34:54,832
A bigfin reef squid is very expensive.

895
00:34:54,833 --> 00:34:57,202
- We can't do that. - But we didn't buy it.

896
00:34:57,203 --> 00:34:58,702
We caught it by fishing.

897
00:34:58,703 --> 00:35:01,073
- A bigfin reef squid in jjajang? - It's unbelievable.

898
00:35:01,313 --> 00:35:03,783
- Is there a cutting board? - Yes, there is.

899
00:35:04,083 --> 00:35:05,523
To be honest, I need to ask someone.

900
00:35:06,113 --> 00:35:07,553
Honey, where is a cutting board?

901
00:35:07,853 --> 00:35:09,012
Where is a cutting board?

902
00:35:09,013 --> 00:35:11,283
- You always go, "Honey." - Honey.

903
00:35:11,653 --> 00:35:12,752
Give it to me.

904
00:35:12,753 --> 00:35:15,192
Jun Haeng doesn't talk much, but he keeps moving.

905
00:35:15,193 --> 00:35:17,833
(The man who's light on his feet was rinsing the octopus quietly.)

906
00:35:18,063 --> 00:35:19,783
Please give me a cutting board and a knife.

907
00:35:20,693 --> 00:35:22,133
I'll bring you a cutting board soon.

908
00:35:22,633 --> 00:35:23,832
(Running over)

909
00:35:23,833 --> 00:35:25,033
He's a giver.

910
00:35:26,333 --> 00:35:27,602
- Thank you. - Thank you.

911
00:35:27,603 --> 00:35:28,973
It's windy. Hold on.

912
00:35:29,573 --> 00:35:31,603
- Is there instant rice? - No.

913
00:35:32,673 --> 00:35:33,913
Honey, is there instant rice?

914
00:35:34,513 --> 00:35:36,393
- There's instant rice. - There's instant rice.

915
00:35:37,043 --> 00:35:39,083
- He's so loving. - I don't know what we have here.

916
00:35:39,183 --> 00:35:41,383
I just know how to drive and dive.

917
00:35:43,023 --> 00:35:44,423
I'm so jealous.

918
00:35:44,753 --> 00:35:46,323
This couple is so cute.

919
00:35:46,523 --> 00:35:48,152
A couple that lives like that...

920
00:35:48,153 --> 00:35:49,762
- by the sea looks nice. - The sea couple is charming, right?

921
00:35:49,763 --> 00:35:51,492
- That's right. They're nice. - The sea couple.

922
00:35:51,493 --> 00:35:53,262
You can go there as the broom-to-be.

923
00:35:53,263 --> 00:35:55,163
- As the broom-to-be? - Yes.

924
00:35:56,033 --> 00:36:00,132
(Bride and groom-to-be)

925
00:36:00,133 --> 00:36:02,103
(Broom-to-be)

926
00:36:02,943 --> 00:36:05,503
- You have a point. Okay. - You can do that.

927
00:36:05,643 --> 00:36:06,813
"Honey, I love you."

928
00:36:07,113 --> 00:36:08,243
"I love you too."

929
00:36:08,673 --> 00:36:10,759
You can do the same thing. "Honey, bring me a cutting board."

930
00:36:10,783 --> 00:36:11,883
Then you can bring it.

931
00:36:12,853 --> 00:36:14,452
After ten years on I Live Alone...

932
00:36:14,453 --> 00:36:15,973
(Broom-to-be can do everything alone.)

933
00:36:16,453 --> 00:36:17,582
You need to turn it on.

934
00:36:17,583 --> 00:36:19,322
- I'll do it. - Give it to me.

935
00:36:19,323 --> 00:36:20,953
(Handing over)

936
00:36:21,523 --> 00:36:24,163
(He hands over the cutting board and begins to set up the burner.)

937
00:36:25,763 --> 00:36:27,833
- Here. - He set everything up.

938
00:36:28,293 --> 00:36:31,003
- Jae Suk cooked a lot. - She cooked.

939
00:36:32,033 --> 00:36:33,372
I bet she's good at cooking.

940
00:36:33,373 --> 00:36:34,903
- Yes, she's good at cooking. - Yes.

941
00:36:35,903 --> 00:36:37,542
(Jang Woo adds the meat he brought from Seoul.)

942
00:36:37,543 --> 00:36:38,943
Gosh, the onions are strong.

943
00:36:40,773 --> 00:36:41,972
It's proper jjajang.

944
00:36:41,973 --> 00:36:43,942
- It's like ganjjajang. - It's nice.

945
00:36:43,943 --> 00:36:45,043
It's like ganjjajang.

946
00:36:45,783 --> 00:36:47,463
- Shall I add the octopus as it is? - Yes.

947
00:36:48,313 --> 00:36:49,552
- Will you boil it as it is? - There you go.

948
00:36:49,553 --> 00:36:50,683
Well done.

949
00:36:51,153 --> 00:36:52,322
It's so thick.

950
00:36:52,323 --> 00:36:54,423
- The knife... - Watch your hands.

951
00:36:54,723 --> 00:36:56,593
- Shall I bring you gloves? - No, it's okay.

952
00:36:56,693 --> 00:36:57,823
He's so sweet.

953
00:36:58,093 --> 00:36:59,192
- Is the knife sharp? - My goodness.

954
00:36:59,193 --> 00:37:01,062
The knife is sharp, but this squid is so thick.

955
00:37:01,063 --> 00:37:02,833
Shall I bring you a bigger knife?

956
00:37:03,203 --> 00:37:04,563
I'll do it.

957
00:37:05,403 --> 00:37:06,473
What? I'll do it.

958
00:37:08,473 --> 00:37:11,473
Did he make a big mistake in the past?

959
00:37:11,903 --> 00:37:13,042
What are you talking about?

960
00:37:13,043 --> 00:37:14,972
- How is that possible? - He's just good to his wife.

961
00:37:14,973 --> 00:37:16,842
- How is that possible? - Seriously.

962
00:37:16,843 --> 00:37:19,242
I've known them for 13 years, right?

963
00:37:19,243 --> 00:37:21,183
- He has always been like that. - You know,

964
00:37:21,213 --> 00:37:24,523
the recipient of such care needs a matching personality.

965
00:37:25,423 --> 00:37:28,053
(After seeing Joy's brother-in-law who was the sweetest man, )

966
00:37:28,393 --> 00:37:30,992
(he passionately talked about his firm belief in marriage.)

967
00:37:30,993 --> 00:37:32,022
You need to meet someone like that.

968
00:37:32,023 --> 00:37:33,562
Someone like me would go, "Leave me be!"

969
00:37:33,563 --> 00:37:35,263
"Don't do anything!"

970
00:37:35,463 --> 00:37:38,062
If the recipient does that, his attentiveness will be wasted.

971
00:37:38,063 --> 00:37:40,272
- They need to be a good match. - It takes teamwork.

972
00:37:40,273 --> 00:37:42,993
- They need to be a good match. - Yes. They need to be a good match.

973
00:37:43,673 --> 00:37:44,803
It's so slippery.

974
00:37:46,973 --> 00:37:49,173
Honey, do you want to do it?

975
00:37:49,373 --> 00:37:50,442
Yes.

976
00:37:50,443 --> 00:37:51,513
(Yes, of course.)

977
00:37:52,613 --> 00:37:53,752
How do you want me to cut it?

978
00:37:53,753 --> 00:37:54,852
Just make it fit into the pot.

979
00:37:54,853 --> 00:37:58,182
- They lead fun lives. - It looks like...

980
00:37:58,183 --> 00:38:00,193
they already have roles to play.

981
00:38:00,453 --> 00:38:02,022
- I love how they use a polite tone. - I think...

982
00:38:02,023 --> 00:38:04,022
Jae Suk can do things by herself.

983
00:38:04,023 --> 00:38:06,563
But she just likes to do them with her husband.

984
00:38:07,763 --> 00:38:10,202
(The man with light feet slices the squid skillfully.)

985
00:38:10,203 --> 00:38:11,263
You're good.

986
00:38:14,033 --> 00:38:15,233
(Oh, my!)

987
00:38:15,773 --> 00:38:17,419
Does the octopus need to be boiled for a long time?

988
00:38:17,443 --> 00:38:18,903
I asked the captain.

989
00:38:19,243 --> 00:38:22,042
He told me not to blanch the octopus like they do in Seoul.

990
00:38:22,043 --> 00:38:23,142
If you boil it for too long, it'll be tough.

991
00:38:23,143 --> 00:38:24,643
- No, but... - No.

992
00:38:25,483 --> 00:38:27,453
Right? KEY, how should you boil it?

993
00:38:27,553 --> 00:38:30,082
You should boil it for 5 or 40 minutes.

994
00:38:30,083 --> 00:38:32,082
- That's right. - It should be over 30 minutes.

995
00:38:32,083 --> 00:38:34,222
- You know the Spanish pulpo dish. - Pulpo.

996
00:38:34,223 --> 00:38:37,023
It's tender because they boil the octopus for an hour at least.

997
00:38:37,793 --> 00:38:38,922
They use boiling water.

998
00:38:38,923 --> 00:38:41,192
But in Korea, people prefer...

999
00:38:41,193 --> 00:38:43,562
- blanching the octopus. - They blanch it.

1000
00:38:43,563 --> 00:38:46,573
- They parboil it. - Koreans prefer a firm texture.

1001
00:38:46,703 --> 00:38:48,302
- You need to boil it for long. - The pulpo dish is tender.

1002
00:38:48,303 --> 00:38:49,403
Yes.

1003
00:38:50,173 --> 00:38:51,603
- It's very slippery, right? - Yes.

1004
00:38:52,603 --> 00:38:53,713
Is it too thick?

1005
00:38:55,143 --> 00:38:56,383
It's good enough.

1006
00:38:56,583 --> 00:38:57,683
It's just right, honey.

1007
00:38:58,213 --> 00:39:00,313
(What a sweet comment!)

1008
00:39:00,583 --> 00:39:02,213
They speak so sweetly to each other.

1009
00:39:02,883 --> 00:39:03,983
Wipe your sweat.

1010
00:39:04,783 --> 00:39:05,853
Thank you.

1011
00:39:06,293 --> 00:39:07,523
I'm so jealous.

1012
00:39:08,023 --> 00:39:10,623
(Jang Woo is absorbed in cooking.)

1013
00:39:11,023 --> 00:39:12,722
(The whelks join the octopus.)

1014
00:39:12,723 --> 00:39:13,863
They're huge.

1015
00:39:14,663 --> 00:39:15,792
They look appetizing.

1016
00:39:15,793 --> 00:39:17,179
It's like chicken and seafood soup.

1017
00:39:17,203 --> 00:39:18,333
- You're right. - Exactly.

1018
00:39:18,833 --> 00:39:20,873
- I'll go and wash it. - Okay.

1019
00:39:21,903 --> 00:39:23,002
(Shall I start cooking the bigfin reef squid jjajang?)

1020
00:39:23,003 --> 00:39:24,173
This is so much fun.

1021
00:39:24,873 --> 00:39:26,003
Is that oyster sauce?

1022
00:39:26,073 --> 00:39:27,173
Oyster sauce.

1023
00:39:27,373 --> 00:39:28,812
Oyster sauce is a must for Chinese food.

1024
00:39:28,813 --> 00:39:30,289
(He adds sugar, another indispensable ingredient.)

1025
00:39:30,313 --> 00:39:33,012
Do you make jjajang like that? That's incredible.

1026
00:39:33,013 --> 00:39:34,152
My goodness.

1027
00:39:34,153 --> 00:39:36,273
