1784
01:06:39,337 --> 01:06:40,817
- It's as heavy as 80t. - He's right.

1785
01:06:41,277 --> 01:06:43,646
As for Kian84... There's a really nimble one.

1786
01:06:43,647 --> 01:06:45,776
Is it Allosaurus? There's one that always runs.

1787
01:06:45,777 --> 01:06:48,286
(They have similar instincts to run.)

1788
01:06:48,287 --> 01:06:49,287
They're similar.

1789
01:06:49,288 --> 01:06:50,787
KEY is close to a Stegosaurus.

1790
01:06:51,587 --> 01:06:53,316
There are the ones that look like a chicken.

1791
01:06:53,317 --> 01:06:55,556
They're a little agile.

1792
01:06:55,557 --> 01:06:57,387
(He's somewhat agile.)

1793
01:06:57,557 --> 01:06:59,997
Sung Woo is close to a Pterosaurs.

1794
01:07:00,557 --> 01:07:02,097
- Among them, - That's right.

1795
01:07:02,297 --> 01:07:04,126
- he's like a Pteranodon. - They're similar.

1796
01:07:04,127 --> 01:07:06,197
- They're similar. Good one. - It suits him.

1797
01:07:06,967 --> 01:07:09,137
Then which dinosaur...

1798
01:07:09,367 --> 01:07:11,676
do you like the most?

1799
01:07:11,677 --> 01:07:13,183
- Gosh, it's a difficult question. - Me?

1800
01:07:13,207 --> 01:07:14,507
I like...

1801
01:07:15,547 --> 01:07:17,717
Well, I like Elasmosaurus.

1802
01:07:18,347 --> 01:07:19,446
- The one with a long neck? - Yes.

1803
01:07:19,447 --> 01:07:20,477
Yes.

1804
01:07:20,617 --> 01:07:21,687
What's the reason?

1805
01:07:22,917 --> 01:07:25,786
Because it has a long neck,

1806
01:07:25,787 --> 01:07:27,727
I found it quite cool.

1807
01:07:28,257 --> 01:07:29,387
I thought it was cool,

1808
01:07:30,627 --> 01:07:32,227
but after looking at its face...

1809
01:07:32,657 --> 01:07:34,697
It's become less likable now.

1810
01:07:36,527 --> 01:07:38,296
- I like it less now, but... - It's less likable now?

1811
01:07:38,297 --> 01:07:39,977
- Yes. - You keep changing your favorites.

1812
01:07:40,837 --> 01:07:43,237
Which dinosaur do you like the most?

1813
01:07:43,467 --> 01:07:45,337
I like Pachycephalosaurus.

1814
01:07:45,737 --> 01:07:46,807
Why?

1815
01:07:47,037 --> 01:07:48,306
It's the cutest because it's small.

1816
01:07:48,307 --> 01:07:49,907
- Because it's cute? - Yes.

1817
01:07:50,847 --> 01:07:53,217
- Is your school on break? - Yes.

1818
01:07:53,777 --> 01:07:55,817
- Which grade are you in? - I'm in the sixth grade.

1819
01:07:56,287 --> 01:07:57,486
Really?

1820
01:07:57,487 --> 01:07:58,957
They're quite small.

1821
01:07:59,757 --> 01:08:01,927
I find small ones cute.

1822
01:08:03,887 --> 01:08:05,847
You're doing something amazing when you're young.

1823
01:08:06,227 --> 01:08:08,227
- Thank you. - It was amazing. Thank you.

1824
01:08:08,867 --> 01:08:10,067
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1825
01:08:10,167 --> 01:08:12,097
I'll go for the Paleozoic era next time.

1826
01:08:12,397 --> 01:08:14,737
- Keep up the good work. - Goodbye.

1827
01:08:17,707 --> 01:08:18,806
Seriously...

1828
01:08:18,807 --> 01:08:20,087
There are so many living things.

1829
01:08:24,517 --> 01:08:26,916
(Heading somewhere after enjoying the exhibition)

1830
01:08:26,917 --> 01:08:28,016
What's this?

1831
01:08:28,017 --> 01:08:29,716
- A merch shop. - Right. A merch shop is a must.

1832
01:08:29,717 --> 01:08:30,817
You must get one.

1833
01:08:32,057 --> 01:08:34,016
(You can't walk past this place.)

1834
01:08:34,017 --> 01:08:35,856
- You must go. - You must buy one.

1835
01:08:35,857 --> 01:08:36,957
That one is cute.

1836
01:08:37,327 --> 01:08:38,656
There are so many things here.

1837
01:08:38,657 --> 01:08:40,557
Gosh, it'd win over kids' hearts.

1838
01:08:42,827 --> 01:08:44,266
(Ju Seung carefully takes a look at each item.)

1839
01:08:44,267 --> 01:08:45,507
You can't walk past this place.

1840
01:08:46,137 --> 01:08:47,167
That's right.

1841
01:08:47,707 --> 01:08:49,767
Wait. This is cute.

1842
01:08:52,977 --> 01:08:55,247
(I'll get this too.)

1843
01:08:56,377 --> 01:08:58,546
They were of such good quality that I kept wanting more.

1844
01:08:58,547 --> 01:09:00,717
Merch from museums these days are quite good.

1845
01:09:01,487 --> 01:09:02,516
Does it glow in the dark?

1846
01:09:02,517 --> 01:09:03,647
(Does it glow in the dark?)

1847
01:09:04,517 --> 01:09:06,056
- I loved luminous items as a kid. - That's right.

1848
01:09:06,057 --> 01:09:07,316
- I love luminous items. - Right.

1849
01:09:07,317 --> 01:09:08,477
Why did we love them so much?

1850
01:09:08,827 --> 01:09:10,087
This must glow in the dark.

1851
01:09:10,557 --> 01:09:11,557
I should get it.

1852
01:09:11,558 --> 01:09:13,956
Gosh, they're quite detailed.

1853
01:09:13,957 --> 01:09:15,067
They're well-made.

1854
01:09:15,967 --> 01:09:18,137
- They're quite nice. - Gosh, they're really long.

1855
01:09:19,797 --> 01:09:21,806
(Grabbing another one)

1856
01:09:21,807 --> 01:09:23,706
My mom used to scold me when I grabbed things like that.

1857
01:09:23,707 --> 01:09:25,007
- Of course. - That's right.

1858
01:09:25,137 --> 01:09:27,707
- You can only get 1 among them. - Right. You can get only 1.

1859
01:09:29,977 --> 01:09:33,247
(Heading to the counter after grabbing a bunch)

1860
01:09:34,247 --> 01:09:35,617
Gosh, seriously...

1861
01:09:36,317 --> 01:09:38,456
I'll get this poster too.

1862
01:09:38,457 --> 01:09:39,916
This is the fun of being a grown-up.

1863
01:09:39,917 --> 01:09:41,933
You feel proud of yourself in such moments as a grown-up.

1864
01:09:41,957 --> 01:09:43,926
It's 108 dollars in total.

1865
01:09:43,927 --> 01:09:45,157
You got a lot of things.

1866
01:09:45,497 --> 01:09:47,866
I couldn't get a dinosaur toy even if I wanted to as a kid.

1867
01:09:47,867 --> 01:09:48,967
Thank you.

1868
01:09:51,337 --> 01:09:52,567
(Looking around)

1869
01:09:53,897 --> 01:09:57,007
(He heads back to the exhibit hall instead of going home?)

1870
01:09:58,037 --> 01:09:59,076
Wait.

1871
01:09:59,077 --> 01:10:00,807
(Where is he going?)

1872
01:10:02,007 --> 01:10:04,247
Sir.

1873
01:10:04,347 --> 01:10:05,547
- Oh, it's a gift? - Yes.

1874
01:10:06,717 --> 01:10:08,247
- I got you a gift. - Sorry?

1875
01:10:09,287 --> 01:10:12,056
Pick the dinosaur you like.

1876
01:10:12,057 --> 01:10:13,587
(A gift of gratitude for the docent)

1877
01:10:14,117 --> 01:10:15,126
(Gosh, it's my favorite dinosaur.)

1878
01:10:15,127 --> 01:10:16,487
- It's a gift. - Thank you.

1879
01:10:16,927 --> 01:10:18,057
(Feeling proud)

1880
01:10:18,397 --> 01:10:20,127
It's a volunteer work for him,

1881
01:10:20,297 --> 01:10:21,817
so I really wanted to give him a gift.

1882
01:10:23,267 --> 01:10:25,167
That's such a cool volunteer job.

1883
01:10:25,237 --> 01:10:27,537
I only did recycling for a year.

1884
01:10:28,967 --> 01:10:30,236
Let's go.

1885
01:10:30,237 --> 01:10:32,877
(Setting off after filling himself with the energy from dinosaurs)

1886
01:10:33,177 --> 01:10:34,677
(Glancing over)

1887
01:10:34,747 --> 01:10:37,177
Gosh, that's a Pterosaur.

1888
01:10:39,347 --> 01:10:40,647
It does look like a Pterosaur.

1889
01:10:40,847 --> 01:10:42,247
- This is really... - You're right.

1890
01:10:43,217 --> 01:10:44,586
Ju Seung can be romantic.

1891
01:10:44,587 --> 01:10:45,787
(Moved)

1892
01:10:47,687 --> 01:10:48,826
What? He's not going home?

1893
01:10:48,827 --> 01:10:51,427
(Romantic Ju Seung heads to somewhere else.)

1894
01:10:52,397 --> 01:10:53,457
Lottery?

1895
01:10:54,627 --> 01:10:55,896
Hello.

1896
01:10:55,897 --> 01:10:57,196
You still want a big win.

1897
01:10:57,197 --> 01:10:59,297
Do you buy lottery tickets often?

1898
01:10:59,537 --> 01:11:01,537
No, not often,

1899
01:11:01,737 --> 01:11:04,237
but on days when you feel small...

1900
01:11:04,267 --> 01:11:06,706
Why? Because the docent was too superior in knowledge?

1901
01:11:06,707 --> 01:11:07,907
No, it's not that.

1902
01:11:07,947 --> 01:11:10,647
After seeing the Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic era,

1903
01:11:10,807 --> 01:11:12,516
you realize we've only lived for four seconds, figuratively.

1904
01:11:12,517 --> 01:11:14,146
- That's right. - So you feel empty.

1905
01:11:14,147 --> 01:11:15,746
- We're only like dust. - I thought I needed...

1906
01:11:15,747 --> 01:11:16,886
dopamine in such moments.

1907
01:11:16,887 --> 01:11:18,716
I go there when I want to cope with emptiness.

1908
01:11:18,717 --> 01:11:19,787
Through lottery?

1909
01:11:19,817 --> 01:11:21,427
(Dumbfounded)

1910
01:11:21,527 --> 01:11:23,927
You wouldn't even look at a dinosaur if you win.

1911
01:11:24,927 --> 01:11:26,527
(Taking something out)

1912
01:11:27,397 --> 01:11:30,027
(He takes something out from the phone case.)

1913
01:11:30,127 --> 01:11:32,137
I'll exchange this for a ticket.

1914
01:11:32,237 --> 01:11:33,697
- To this? - Yes.

1915
01:11:33,967 --> 01:11:35,487
- Oh, my. What is that? - What's that?

1916
01:11:35,807 --> 01:11:36,807
You won?

1917
01:11:36,937 --> 01:11:38,736
- Really? - The tickets I won.

1918
01:11:38,737 --> 01:11:40,783
(Repurchasing lottery tickets with the 9 dollars he won)

1919
01:11:40,807 --> 01:11:43,347
(He secures a seat and sits down.)

1920
01:11:44,677 --> 01:11:45,677
Please...

1921
01:11:46,747 --> 01:11:47,817
Let's see.

1922
01:11:48,187 --> 01:11:49,487
If there's a number...

1923
01:11:49,817 --> 01:11:51,687
that matches the lucky number...

1924
01:11:52,587 --> 01:11:54,587
(The lucky number is 7.)

1925
01:11:55,257 --> 01:11:57,827
(He wins if he gets another 7.)

1926
01:11:58,227 --> 01:11:59,696
There is one!

1927
01:11:59,697 --> 01:12:00,697
- What is? - What is it?

1928
01:12:01,567 --> 01:12:02,697
I won from the first round?

1929
01:12:03,227 --> 01:12:04,297
Seven.

1930
01:12:04,497 --> 01:12:06,036
Oh, number seven? You win if there is one.

1931
01:12:06,037 --> 01:12:07,596
- He won one dollar. - Gosh.

1932
01:12:07,597 --> 01:12:09,513
- I won 1 dollar. - You get 2 dollars if there are 2?

1933
01:12:09,537 --> 01:12:11,136
- No, you must scratch to find out. - Oh, my. It's one dollar.

1934
01:12:11,137 --> 01:12:12,206
Oh, it's written down.

1935
01:12:12,207 --> 01:12:13,306
The price is written.

1936
01:12:13,307 --> 01:12:15,177
- Oh, the price is written? - That's right.

1937
01:12:15,377 --> 01:12:16,576
Gosh, how amazing.

1938
01:12:16,577 --> 01:12:18,537
(He keeps the momentum going to the next ticket.)

1939
01:12:19,017 --> 01:12:22,447
(I need to get number 5.)

1940
01:12:23,917 --> 01:12:25,387
You lost. Goodness.

1941
01:12:27,057 --> 01:12:30,257
(He loses in a row, failing to fill the emptiness.)

1942
01:12:31,827 --> 01:12:34,327
(This time, he pays more effort into it.)

1943
01:12:36,927 --> 01:12:38,937
- Gosh, it's eight. - He won.

1944
01:12:39,067 --> 01:12:40,747
- What? How much is it? - Yes, he won two.

1945
01:12:44,307 --> 01:12:45,336
Darn it.

1946
01:12:45,337 --> 01:12:47,106
(He won a dollar again.)

1947
01:12:47,107 --> 01:12:48,647
(A dollar)

1948
01:12:49,207 --> 01:12:50,806
(It's a shame that it's only a dollar.)

1949
01:12:50,807 --> 01:12:53,277
- It was only a dollar. - It does give you a dopamine rush.

1950
01:12:54,117 --> 01:12:55,986
I think he'll win about five dollars.

1951
01:12:55,987 --> 01:12:57,087
I bet he'll win ten.

1952
01:12:58,657 --> 01:12:59,657
Gosh.

1953
01:13:00,617 --> 01:13:02,557
I'd win 500,000 dollars if I get either 2 or 6.

1954
01:13:02,727 --> 01:13:04,486
- Gosh. - It says 500,000 dollars.

1955
01:13:04,487 --> 01:13:05,826
Do you win 500,000 dollars if you get 6?

1956
01:13:05,827 --> 01:13:07,796
- I win 500,000 dollars if I get 6. - That's right.

1957
01:13:07,797 --> 01:13:08,917
Oh, that's the lucky number.

1958
01:13:09,127 --> 01:13:10,197
Six.

1959
01:13:10,667 --> 01:13:11,767
Five or six.

1960
01:13:12,537 --> 01:13:13,967
That's very intuitive.

1961
01:13:13,997 --> 01:13:15,413
It gives you the biggest dopamine rush.

1962
01:13:15,437 --> 01:13:17,207
Right. You can find out right away.

1963
01:13:17,567 --> 01:13:18,807
(Desperate)

1964
01:13:18,837 --> 01:13:20,036
Saurus.

1965
01:13:20,037 --> 01:13:21,747
(Please let me win.)

1966
01:13:24,147 --> 01:13:25,417
Please... What?

1967
01:13:25,677 --> 01:13:26,917
What is it?

1968
01:13:27,217 --> 01:13:28,317
Is it round?

1969
01:13:28,617 --> 01:13:30,947
(Is it number 5, the number he desperately wanted?)

1970
01:13:32,417 --> 01:13:34,687
(This is nerve-racking.)

1971
01:13:35,187 --> 01:13:37,157
(Here we go!)

1972
01:13:37,527 --> 01:13:38,557
What?

1973
01:13:38,727 --> 01:13:39,796
- Isn't that five? - Five?

1974
01:13:39,797 --> 01:13:42,077
- I win 20,000 dollars if it's 5. - Gosh, 20,000 dollars.

1975
01:13:42,467 --> 01:13:43,527
What?

1976
01:13:43,797 --> 01:13:45,337
(Please...)

1977
01:13:45,589 --> 01:13:46,589
What?

1978
01:13:46,717 --> 01:13:47,746
- Isn't that five? - Five?

1979
01:13:47,747 --> 01:13:50,027
- I win 20,000 dollars if it's 5. - Gosh, 20,000 dollars.

1980
01:13:51,742 --> 01:13:53,942
(It's 3, not 5.)

1981
01:13:54,412 --> 01:13:56,181
- Still, you won five dollars. - Five dollars.

1982
01:13:56,182 --> 01:13:57,411
- It's five dollars. - Seriously.

1983
01:13:57,412 --> 01:13:58,822
- That's not bad. - Nice.

1984
01:13:59,052 --> 01:14:00,282
If it's five dollars...

1985
01:14:00,482 --> 01:14:01,552
You broke even.

1986
01:14:01,692 --> 01:14:03,791
I won 7 dollars after spending 9.

1987
01:14:03,792 --> 01:14:05,462
- That's not bad. - I won seven dollars.

1988
01:14:05,662 --> 01:14:07,891
You used two dollars for the dopamine rush.

1989
01:14:07,892 --> 01:14:08,992
It's not bad.

1990
01:14:09,532 --> 01:14:11,362
Gosh, this isn't easy.

1991
01:14:11,932 --> 01:14:14,572
(I wanted to fill dopamine as I felt empty.)

1992
01:14:14,672 --> 01:14:16,472
Can I exchange it for the Lotto ticket?

1993
01:14:16,732 --> 01:14:17,971
You can't find out the result right away...

1994
01:14:17,972 --> 01:14:18,972
if you get the Lotto ticket.

1995
01:14:18,973 --> 01:14:21,172
- I see. - You spent all the money.

1996
01:14:22,012 --> 01:14:23,972
I feel like the store owner is holding his laugh.

1997
01:14:24,542 --> 01:14:25,862
Have a good day. I'll come again.

1998
01:14:26,812 --> 01:14:29,382
I could win Lotto.

1999
01:14:29,952 --> 01:14:31,952
So what happened to your Lotto ticket?

2000
01:14:32,282 --> 01:14:33,921
- I didn't win any. - Goodness.

2001
01:14:33,922 --> 01:14:35,352
He wouldn't be here had he won.

2002
01:14:35,592 --> 01:14:37,692
(That's not true.)

2003
01:14:38,962 --> 01:14:43,092
(The time he hovered between his dream and the secular world)

2004
01:14:43,892 --> 01:14:45,762
(Ju Seung returned home.)

2005
01:14:46,762 --> 01:14:48,531
Gosh, I haven't seen Ju Seung's house for a while.

2006
01:14:48,532 --> 01:14:50,101
He still has the same lights.

2007
01:14:50,102 --> 01:14:51,772
- The house where I pooped. - Exactly.

2008
01:14:52,642 --> 01:14:53,842
Gosh, I'm so hungry.

2009
01:14:55,272 --> 01:14:57,742
(He became hungry after doing a study on dinosaurs.)

2010
01:14:58,142 --> 01:14:59,411
What is he going to eat?

2011
01:14:59,412 --> 01:15:01,412
Is he going to eat something related to dinosaurs?

2012
01:15:01,482 --> 01:15:02,912
(Thinking)

2013
01:15:03,282 --> 01:15:04,562
Something like Yonggari chicken.

2014
01:15:05,582 --> 01:15:06,681
- That'd be amazing. - Right?

2015
01:15:06,682 --> 01:15:07,822
That'd complete his day.

2016
01:15:07,892 --> 01:15:10,492
- That way, it's consistent. - Yes. Yonggari chicken sounds nice.

2017
01:15:12,322 --> 01:15:14,261
(Did he take out Yonggari chicken from the freezer?)

2018
01:15:14,262 --> 01:15:16,102
- Oh, it needs to be thawed. - Buckwheat soup?

2019
01:15:20,302 --> 01:15:22,271
You use the single-handle pot well.

2020
01:15:22,272 --> 01:15:23,971
- It's really convenient. - There's nothing like it.

2021
01:15:23,972 --> 01:15:25,241
That's the best.

2022
01:15:25,242 --> 01:15:26,771
(When the water starts to boil...)

2023
01:15:26,772 --> 01:15:28,272
(Biting)

2024
01:15:29,142 --> 01:15:30,918
- Is it naengmyeon? - It's buckwheat noodles.

2025
01:15:30,942 --> 01:15:32,082
That looks delicious.

2026
01:15:32,642 --> 01:15:35,012
(After stirring it carefully so it wouldn't stick, )

2027
01:15:36,382 --> 01:15:38,051
(he washes it in cold water.)

2028
01:15:38,052 --> 01:15:40,132
Buckwheat noodles are the best on hot days like now.

2029
01:15:41,522 --> 01:15:43,121
The buckwheat noodles I made...

2030
01:15:43,122 --> 01:15:44,661
isn't an ordinary buckwheat noodle dish.

2031
01:15:44,662 --> 01:15:45,692
I see.

2032
01:15:48,292 --> 01:15:49,332
That's set.

2033
01:15:51,302 --> 01:15:53,102
(He opens the fridge again.)

2034
01:15:54,362 --> 01:15:56,171
(What is that?)

2035
01:15:56,172 --> 01:15:57,801
- What? Watermelon? - What is that?

2036
01:15:57,802 --> 01:15:59,001
I think watermelon would suit it.

2037
01:15:59,002 --> 01:16:01,442
What? You're adding it?

2038
01:16:02,872 --> 01:16:05,011
- What is he doing? - That's a new combination.

2039
01:16:05,012 --> 01:16:06,911
I can't imagine the taste at all.

2040
01:16:06,912 --> 01:16:09,711
But there are restaurants that add watermelon to soybean noodles.

2041
01:16:09,712 --> 01:16:11,252
Yes, there are.

2042
01:16:11,382 --> 01:16:13,102
But to add watermelon to buckwheat noodles?

2043
01:16:13,152 --> 01:16:15,291
It's cold watermelon buckwheat noodles.

2044
01:16:15,292 --> 01:16:16,491
